INV-jurif's env-read discriminator is "a variable expansion whose name is never
ASSIGNED in this file". That is right for a local and wrong for the
conditional-default idiom, one of the commonest ways a shell script reads its
environment: test whether the environment supplied the name, assign a FALLBACK
only when it did not.

    gocryptfs/test.bash:17   if [[ -z ${TMPDIR:-} ]]; then TMPDIR=/var/tmp; fi

Read through the whole-file rule, the fallback on line 18 is proof TMPDIR is a
local. It is the opposite: `${VAR:-x}` means "if VAR is unset or null, use x",
so writing it is the script declaring the value may arrive from outside.

All eight default / alternate / error operators count (:- - := = :? ? :+ +).
The TRANSFORMING forms deliberately do not -- ${VAR#p}, ${VAR%p}, ${VAR/a/b},
${#VAR} operate on a value the script already holds and say nothing about its
origin; treating them as evidence of external supply would make every local
string manipulation a taint source.

ONE HOME. The subtraction lives inside _assigned_names, which its own docstring
names as the single home, because the edge extractor and the repo source-join
index both consult it and a second copy is how the two drift into calling
different things an environment read. Read off `expansion` NODES rather than
raw text, so a ${VAR:-} inside a comment cannot mint a source.

